  <Patch Id="ErO4A39RrERK8YaQPcfmVQ">
    <Canvas Id="H4w2j0GR20FC7OAgU3du8v" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="C41nxKXC0YypJzm5lMYwDB">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="A9VxtIWIi6CLaqGT3YTKHo">
        <Canvas Id="T9w8alvHq6b5KSmj6tpF8J" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="HDbR3HCv6XfUlwAWwNwFYp" Bounds="-769,-73,620,1068" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="01 - Your cursor is now data.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Two windows. Move the mouse over the LEFT one: the dot follows, and WRITE WKT spells your&#xD;&#xA;position as a VALUE - POINT (0.42 0.77) - in the renderer's own canvas units. Move it over&#xD;&#xA;the RIGHT one: the same cursor becomes POINT (139.7 35.6) - longitude and latitude - because&#xD;&#xA;that window is a MAP looking at Tokyo. No tiles, no internet: a map is an agreement about&#xD;&#xA;coordinates before it is a picture.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;SAME CURSOR, TWO COORDINATE SYSTEMS, TWO NUMBERS - and neither is more true. The window's&#xD;&#xA;PIXELS are the parent both are computed from: Skia turns pixels into canvas units (Mouse),&#xD;&#xA;the map turns pixels into degrees (Console -&gt; MouseState -&gt; ScreenToWorld). A position is&#xD;&#xA;two numbers PLUS the agreement about what they mean. Hold on to that - chapters 08 and 10&#xD;&#xA;are built on it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Coordinate / Point   geometry's raw material - and geometry does not care whose numbers&#xD;&#xA;                       it holds. The SAME nodes carry canvas units on the left and degrees&#xD;&#xA;                       on the right.&#xD;&#xA;  WKT                  the spelling every GIS tool reads. Both sides speak it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Copy either WKT text into a chat or a file. Your dot travels as text.&#xD;&#xA;  Move the mouse straight down: canvas Y GROWS, latitude SHRINKS - the two systems even&#xD;&#xA;  disagree about which way is up.&#xD;&#xA;  Change the Map's Initial pins and reopen the patch: Initial means once, at first sight.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  coordinate system   the agreement that turns two numbers into a position&#xD;&#xA;  WGS84               the lon/lat agreement the map side speaks&#xD;&#xA;  X first             GIS says east before north - the opposite of spoken 'lat, long',&#xD;&#xA;                      and the most common bug in the whole domain&#xD;&#xA;  WKT                  Well-Known Text, the spelling every tool reads and writes&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 02   dots become paths and plots of ground&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 08   the map starts answering back - this chapter's right half, grown up&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 10   same place, different numbers - why neither side is more true">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
